<analysis>
The get_user function could potentially be vulnerable to a potential SQL injection. The query in this function directly concatenates the access token into the SQL statement without any sanitization or parameterisation, which would make it susceptible for SQL injections. An attacker can input malicious SQL code that gets executed server-side and may lead to data leakage or even data manipulation depending on the privileges of the user whose session is being hijacked.
Moreover, there's another potential security issue: This function appears to cache a database query result (`self._user`) within an instance variable. If this code runs in a multi-threaded environment where simultaneous requests are being processed by different threads on the same instance of `get_user`, then concurrent access could potentially lead to race conditions that might not be exploitable in all cases but could cause unexpected behaviour or data corruption.
</analysis>

