<analysis>
This code snippet has an SQL injection vulnerability. The `%s` format string in the SQL query is susceptible to arbitrary Python object injections that can manipulate this part of the SQL command, resulting in unauthorized data access or modification. It could be exploited by sending crafted HTTP requests to directly inject malicious SQL commands into the 'sloka_number' parameter.

Moreover, string formatting without any sanitization is used to construct the `sloka_number_previous` and `sloka_number_next` which could lead to insecure direct object references (IDOR). A user can manipulate this part of the request URL to access data that they are not supposed to have access to.

In general, SQL commands should be constructed using parameterized queries or prepared statements to avoid such vulnerabilities. This would also make it easier to prevent SQL injection attacks in the future by limiting where and how input can influence these commands. 

Additionally, this code appears to expose sensitive data without proper authorization checks which could present a potential information disclosure risk if not properly secured.
</analysis>

