- `my @list = qw(apple banana orange);`: This line creates an array named '@list' and initializes it with three strings - 'apple', 'banana', and 'orange'. The 'qw()' function is a shorthand for quoting words. It automatically adds whitespace around the input, which means "apple", "banana" and "orange" will be treated as separate elements in array @list.
- `foreach my $element (@list) {`: This line starts a 'foreach loop' that iterates over each element in the @list array. The variable $element is automatically set to the value of each item during each iteration. 
- `print "$element\n";`: In this line, it prints out the current $element on screen followed by an endline character. "\n" makes a newline character, causing the output to have one element per line. 
- The script ends without any explicit return statement as Perl scripts implicitly return the value of the last expression evaluated. In this case that is also what 'print' returns - number of characters printed which in our case would be always 0 for all elements as we are printing a string followed by a newline character.


