1. The ARR_SIZE macro seems to be a compiler-specific way of getting array size at compile time. While it may help with some safety checks and preventing out-of-bounds access, there is no guarantee for the 'sorted' state of this array if the function isn't invoked again after being used.
2. The code block that sorts the insn_regs_intel (if not already sorted) might be expensive in terms of execution time. This sorting could be moved outside the scope of X86_insn_reg_intel or run only when a new instruction set changes.
3. If there are more elements with similar 'insn' values, binary search may result in accessing wrong element as it does not return the first occurrence.
4. The memcpy and qsort calls could be made static to avoid unnecessary memory allocations every time this function is called. 
5. It would be beneficial to add a guard condition around mid to prevent an infinite loop caused by a malicious input. This can be done by asserting that first <= last before the while-loop begins, or just returning 0 if it's not.
</analysis>

